To modify the given C++ code to generate a list of the ten most used keywords in an article titled "The Future of AI in Healthcare" using Markdown, you can follow these steps:
1. Read the article from a Markdown file and store it in a string variable.
2. Parse the Markdown file to extract the text content and store it in the same string variable as step 1.
3. Remove all the Markdown formatting tags from the string variable.
4. Tokenize the string into individual words and store them in a vector.
5. Convert all the words to lowercase to avoid case sensitivity.
6. Create a map to store the frequency of each keyword.
7. Iterate through the vector of words and update the frequency of each keyword in the map.
8. Create a set to store the top ten keywords based on their frequency.
9. Print the top ten keywords.
Here is the modified code:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <set>
#include <regex>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
    // Read the article from a Markdown file and store it in a string variable
    ifstream file("article.md");
    string article((istreambuf_iterator<char>(file)), istreambuf_iterator<char>());
    // Parse the Markdown file to extract the text content and store it in the same string variable
    regex mdRegex("(?<=^|\n)([^#*]+)(?=\n#|$)");
    smatch mdMatch;
    while (regex_search(article, mdMatch, mdRegex))
    {
        article = mdMatch.str();
    }
    // Remove all the Markdown formatting tags from the string variable
    regex tagRegex("[*#]");
    article = regex_replace(article, tagRegex, "");
    // Tokenize the string into individual words and store them in a vector
    vector<string> words;
    regex wordRegex("\\b\\w+\\b");
    transform(sregex_iterator(article.begin(), article.end(), wordRegex),
              sregex_iterator(),
              back_inserter(words),
              [](auto match) { return match.str(); });
    // Convert all the words to lowercase to avoid case sensitivity
    transform(words.begin(), words.end(), words.begin(), [](auto word) {
        transform(word.begin(), word.end(), word.begin(), ::tolower);
        return word;
    });
    // Create a map to store the frequency of each keyword
    map<string, int> keywordFrequency;
    for (auto word : words)
    {
        keywordFrequency[word]++;
    }
    // Create a set to store the top ten keywords based on their frequency
    auto cmp = [](const pair<string, int>& a, const pair<string, int>& b) { return a.second > b.second; };
    set<pair<string, int>, decltype(cmp)> topTenKeywords(keywordFrequency.begin(), keywordFrequency.end(), cmp);
    // Print the top ten keywords
    cout << "The top ten keywords in the article are: " << endl;
    for (auto it = topTenKeywords.begin(); it != topTenKeywords.end() && distance(topTenKeywords.begin(), it) < 10; it++)
    {
        cout << "- " << it->first << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
Note: This code assumes that the Markdown file contains only one article and its title is the first heading in the file. If there are multiple articles or the title is not the first heading, you may need to modify the regular expressions used in this code to extract the correct content.

To change the brightness of an image in Java, you can use the BufferedImage class and the Color class. Here's an example program that changes the brightness of an image:
```
import java.awt.Color;
import java.awt.image.BufferedImage;
import java.io.File;
import javax.imageio.ImageIO;
public class ImageBrightness {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            // Load the image
            File input = new File("input.jpg");
            BufferedImage image = ImageIO.read(input);
            // Set the brightness factor
            float brightnessFactor = 1.5f;
            // Loop through each pixel and adjust the brightness
            for (int y = 0; y < image.getHeight(); y++) {
                for (int x = 0; x < image.getWidth(); x++) {
                    // Get the color of the pixel
                    Color c = new Color(image.getRGB(x, y));
                    // Adjust the brightness
                    int red = (int) (c.getRed() * brightnessFactor);
                    int green = (int) (c.getGreen() * brightnessFactor);
                    int blue = (int) (c.getBlue() * brightnessFactor);
                    // Make sure the color values are within the valid range
                    red = Math.min(red, 255);
                    green = Math.min(green, 255);
                    blue = Math.min(blue, 255);
                    // Set the new color of the pixel
                    Color newColor = new Color(red, green, blue);
                    image.setRGB(x, y, newColor.getRGB());
                }
            }
            // Save the modified image
            File output = new File("output.jpg");
            ImageIO.write(image, "jpg", output);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```
In this program, we first load the image from a file using the `ImageIO.read()` method. We then set the brightness factor to 1.5, which will increase the brightness of the image. You can adjust this value to achieve the desired effect.
We then loop through each pixel of the image and adjust the brightness of the color using the brightness factor. We make sure that the color values are within the valid range of 0-255, and then set the new color of the pixel using the `image.setRGB()` method.
Finally, we save the modified image to a file using the `ImageIO.write()` method.
Note that this program assumes that the input image is in JPEG format. You can modify the program to support other image formats by changing the file extension in the `ImageIO.read()` and `ImageIO.write()` methods.

