Sure, here's an example C# code that uses Accord.NET to train and implement the Naive Bayes algorithm for text classification:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using Accord.IO;
using Accord.MachineLearning;
using Accord.MachineLearning.Text;
namespace TextClassification
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Load the labeled dataset
            var data = new CsvReader("labeled_data.csv", hasHeaders: true).ToArrays();
            // Split the dataset into training and testing sets
            var split = new Split(data, testPortion: 0.3);
            var trainingData = split.TrainingSet.ToArray();
            var testingData = split.TestingSet.ToArray();
            // Transform the dataset into a matrix of word counts
            var vectorizer = new CountVectorizer(stopWords: "english");
            var inputs = vectorizer.Transform(trainingData.Select(x => x[0]).ToArray());
            var outputs = trainingData.Select(x => x[1]).ToArray();
            // Train a Naive Bayes classifier on the training set
            var classifier = new NaiveBayes();
            classifier.Learn(inputs, outputs);
            // Evaluate the classifier on the testing set
            var testInputs = vectorizer.Transform(testingData.Select(x => x[0]).ToArray());
            var predicted = classifier.Decide(testInputs);
            var actual = testingData.Select(x => x[1]).ToArray();
            var accuracy = new GeneralConfusionMatrix(actual, predicted).Accuracy;
            Console.WriteLine($"Accuracy: {accuracy}");
            // Classify a new sentence
            var sentence = "The sports game was exciting";
            var sentenceInput = vectorizer.Transform(new[] { sentence });
            var label = classifier.Decide(sentenceInput)[0];
            Console.WriteLine($"Label: {label}");
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, we first load the labeled dataset from a CSV file and split it into training and testing sets. We then use the CountVectorizer class to transform the training set into a matrix of word counts, which is used to train a Naive Bayes classifier. We evaluate the accuracy of the classifier on the testing set and output it to the console.
