    exit;
}
// Perform database operations
// ...
// Close the database connection
mysqli_close($conn);
```
In this example, we first connect to the database using the mysqli_connect function. We then check if the connection was successful using the !$conn syntax. If the connection fails, we log the error message to a table called "error_log" using an SQL INSERT statement. We then display a user-friendly error message to the user and exit the script.
If the connection is successful, we can perform database operations as needed. Finally, we close the database connection using the mysqli_close function.

