This group includes organisms that produce their own energy through photosynthesis or chemosynthesis. They use energy from the sun or chemical reactions to create food for themselves. An example organism in this category is a plant, which uses photosynthesis to convert sunlight into energy.
Category 2: Energy Consumers
This group includes organisms that obtain energy by consuming other organisms. They can be predators or scavengers, and they rely on other organisms for their survival. An example organism in this category is a lion, which hunts and eats other animals to obtain energy.
Category 3: Energy Exchangers
This group includes organisms that exchange energy with their environment in unique ways. They may use symbiotic relationships to obtain energy, or they may rely on environmental factors such as temperature or pressure to survive. An example organism in this category is a coral, which relies on photosynthetic algae living within their tissues to obtain energy.
